# Known differences for an engine matrix run:
#   KNOWN_FILE=corlogic.known.engines
# The line forms are the ones corlogic.known documents.
#
# Only InnoDB indexes a virtual column, so MyISAM and Aria refuse the same CREATE or
# ALTER with ER_KEY_BASED_ON_GENERATED_VIRTUAL_COLUMN.
ERROR|0v1904|*|*
ERROR|1904v0|*|*

# How much a table may hold is the engine's own limit: MEMORY stops at
# max_heap_table_size with ER_RECORD_FILE_FULL where a disk engine carries on.
ERROR|0v1114|*|*
ERROR|1114v0|*|*

# MDEV-40874. An UPDATE that writes a column back to the value it already holds counts one
# affected row on Aria and none on InnoDB. As described in corlogic.known.INFO.
AFFECTED|0v1|UPDATE|UPDATE tX SET cX=DEFAULT

# InnoDB refuses ROW_FORMAT=COMPRESSED and KEY_BLOCK_SIZE while innodb_read_only_compressed
# is ON, the default, with ER_UNSUPPORTED_COMPRESSED_TABLE; MyISAM and Aria take the same CREATE.
ERROR|4047v*|*|*
ERROR|*v4047|*|*

# MyISAM and Aria run MATCH ... AGAINST (... IN BOOLEAN MODE) without a FULLTEXT index; InnoDB
# needs the index and refuses with ER_FT_MATCHING_KEY_NOT_FOUND.
ERROR|1191v0|*|*
ERROR|0v1191|*|*

# Aria takes part in a transaction but keeps no savepoints, so SAVEPOINT in a transaction that
# used an Aria table fails with ER_CHECK_NOT_IMPLEMENTED.
ERROR|1178v0|*|SAVEPOINT*
ERROR|0v1178|*|SAVEPOINT*

# InnoDB adds a VIRTUAL column in place and does not compute it for the rows already there.
# MyISAM and Aria copy the table and compute the column, so a value that does not fit the
# column type fails the ALTER in strict mode.
ERROR|0v*|*ALTER*|*VIRTUAL*
ERROR|*v0*|*ALTER*|*VIRTUAL*

# Each engine reads its own table options and checks them its own way, so the same CREATE or
# ALTER can stop with ER_CANT_CREATE_TABLE on one side, be refused as ER_UNKNOWN_OPTION on
# another, and be taken on a third. PAGE_COMPRESSED, KEY_BLOCK_SIZE, ROW_FORMAT, TRANSACTIONAL
# and ENCRYPTION_KEY_ID are the usual ones.
ERROR|1005v*|*|*
ERROR|*v1005|*|*
ERROR|0v1911|*|*
ERROR|1911v0|*|*

# STRICT_TRANS_TABLES makes a bad value an error for a transactional engine and a warning for a
# non-transactional one, so the same write stops on InnoDB and goes through on MyISAM and Aria.
# As described in corlogic.known.INFO.
ERROR|1292v0|*|*
ERROR|0v1292|*|*

# The longest key an engine takes is its own limit: 3072 bytes on InnoDB, 1000 on MyISAM and
# Aria, so the same CREATE or ALTER stops with ER_TOO_LONG_KEY on one side only.
ERROR|1071v*|*|*
ERROR|*v1071|*|*

# A condition is only evaluated when the statement reaches a row, and how soon an engine
# reports an empty table decides that, so an error inside the condition, such as
# ER_SUBQUERY_NO_1_ROW, comes out on one side only.
ERROR|1242v*|*|*
ERROR|*v1242|*|*
